GPU-MEMORY OOM LEAK (singleplayer terrain demo, 'physics frozen + RAM grows until OOM') ROOT CAUSE + FIX (commit 82578361). DIAGNOSED from the user's in-page [LEAK] probe (app.js animate once/sec window.__leak) + two DevTools traces: JS heap oscillated 10-44MB (GC'd, NOT growing), DOM nodes 429 + listeners 155 + sceneNodes 265 all STABLE -> the climbing 'RAM' is GPU memory, not JS. The [LEAK] console log showed the smoking gun: 'TerrainBackdrop.js planet init failed -> Failed to fetch' (a TRANSIENT network blip on initMapspinnerPlanet's module-relative ./shaders/*.glsl + ../textures/*.jpg - they ARE deployed, all 200) dropped to the no-op fallback backdrop (which does NOT set window.__terrain), AND THEN _buildWorldScenery STILL built vegetation on the flat fallback frame -> the veg InstancedMesh2 ShaderMaterial failed to compile ('THREE.WebGLProgram: Shader Error VALIDATE_STATUS false / Vertex shader is not compiled') and Three.js RE-CREATED the WebGLProgram EVERY renderer.render(scene,camera) -> leaked GPU program objects until OOM, sim appearing frozen as the render starved. FIX: (1) gate veg/rocks/grass BUILD on window.__terrain!=null (planet actually initialized) in _buildWorldScenery - matches the animate loop's existing .update gate; planet-failed => no foliage built => no broken meshes re-compiling on render. (2) retry initMapspinnerPlanet 3x w/ backoff before fallback so a transient 'Failed to fetch' recovers the planet (and re-enables foliage). LESSONS: (a) stable JS heap + growing RAM == GPU/texture/program leak - check renderer.info + WebGLProgram churn, not just performance.memory; (b) a failed-to-compile ShaderMaterial that stays in the scene is re-compiled every frame = unbounded GPU leak - never leave broken materials in the render list; (c) the earlier texture-size cuts + snapshot-coalescing were NOT this leak (heap/postMessage were fine); the in-page [LEAK] probe on USER hardware was what finally pinned it (dev iGPU never loads the world past the build). [[singleplayer-snapshot-postmessage-oom-leak]] was a separate real fix.